# Home lab node registry — hybrid provider (ADR-046 §13)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Format (one line per Flatcar Hyper-V worker node):
#   <hostname>|<ssh-target>|<os-info>|<tailnet-host>|<box-tag>|<target-cluster>|<startupGB>|<minGB>|<maxGB>|<cpus>|<diskGB>
#
# Fields:
#   hostname      : kubernetes node hostname (derived by the provisioner as
#                   flatcar-node-<idx>; the first field is informational)
#   ssh-target    : user@ip or user@hostname for SSH access from the Mac to the
#                   Windows host (provision-flatcar-worker.sh drives Hyper-V
#                   over PowerShell via this SSH session)
#   os-info       : legacy field, informational only
#   tailnet-host  : Tailscale MagicDNS hostname (derived as
#                   flatcar-node-<idx>.<tailnet>; informational)
#   box-tag       : human label for the physical box (box-a, box-b, etc.)
#   target-cluster: destination cluster: 'hub' (Hub management cluster) or
#                   'spoke' (Spoke tenant cluster).
#   startupGB     : VM startup memory in GB (default Hyper-V sizing, auto)
#   minGB         : dynamic memory floor in GB (min==startup==max => static)
#   maxGB         : dynamic memory ceiling in GB
#   cpus          : virtual CPU count. OVERRIDES the provisioner's automatic
#                   per-host division, so set it deliberately or leave the host
#                   to be divided evenly.
#
#                   Sizing matters more than it looks (ADR-046 §24.5). These are
#                   thin laptops: giving every VM the host's full thread count
#                   oversubscribes it, and a 15W part driven at 2:1 reaches the
#                   ACPI critical thermal trip and powers the whole box off
#                   mid-provision. Sum the cpus column across all VMs on a
#                   box-tag and keep it at or near the host's logical CPU count.
#
#                   Do not simply divide evenly either: the hub node carries the
#                   platform (~58 pods, ~1840m of requests) and needs more than
#                   the spoke. Under-sizing it is its own outage — at 2 vCPU the
#                   hub could not schedule Infisical, which took down the
#                   ClusterSecretStore and every ExternalSecret on the hub.
#
#                   Worked example, 2-core/4-thread box-a: hub 3, spoke 2.
#
# Capacity is read from this registry by default — no CLI flags required;
# the provisioner is driven solely by `--node N` (N = 1-based registry index).
#
# IMPORTANT: Copy this file to home-lab.env and fill in real values.
# home-lab.env is gitignored; this example ships safe placeholders.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Box A: Primary workstation (Dual-VM or Dedicated Hub Worker)
HOME_WORKER_NODES="flatcar-hub-node-1|user@REPLACE_IP|unused|flatcar-hub-node-1.REPLACE_TAILNET.ts.net|box-a|hub|8|8|8|3"

# Box A or Box B: Spoke workload worker
HOME_WORKER_NODES="$HOME_WORKER_NODES
flatcar-spoke-node-1|user@REPLACE_IP|unused|flatcar-spoke-node-1.REPLACE_TAILNET.ts.net|box-a|spoke|4|4|4|2"

# Box B: Secondary workstation (Spoke workload worker)
HOME_WORKER_NODES="$HOME_WORKER_NODES
flatcar-spoke-node-2|user@REPLACE_IP|unused|flatcar-spoke-node-2.REPLACE_TAILNET.ts.net|box-b|spoke|4|4|4|2"

# Tailscale tailnet name (e.g. example.ts.net)
TAILNET_NAME="REPLACE_TAILNET.ts.net"

# Hybrid spoke pool name (matches the SpokePool claim name)
HYBRID_SPOKE_NAME="spoke-pool-hybrid-dev-01"

# Hub kubeconfig path (used by the provisioner to read spoke kubeconfig)
# NOTE: The hybrid dev hub is "hub-hybrid-dev.kubeconfig" — "hub.kubeconfig"
# does not exist. Use whatever kubeconfig file was emitted for the environment.
# Respect a caller-supplied value: the bootstrap orchestrator runs this script
# before pivot has written the canonical kubeconfig, and needs to point it
# elsewhere. Unconditional assignment here silently overrides that.
HUB_KUBECONFIG="${HUB_KUBECONFIG:-${ZERO_OPS_DIR:-$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)}/k8-secrets/kubeconfig/hub-hybrid-dev.kubeconfig}"

-------------------------

HOME_WORKER_NODES="
flatcar-hub-node-1|Dell@192.168.X.XX|Ubuntu-24.04|flatcar-hub-node-1.tailaXXXX.ts.net|box-a|hub|8|8|8|3
flatcar-spoke-node-1|Dell@192.168.X.XX|Ubuntu-24.04|flatcar-spoke-node-1.tailaXXXX.ts.net|box-a|spoke|4|4|4|2
flatcar-spoke-node-2|LENOVO@192.168.X.XX|unused|flatcar-spoke-node-2.tailaXXXX.ts.net|box-b|spoke|4|4|4|2
"

# Box B: Lenovo workstation (8 GB RAM, i7-3632QM 4C/8T, Windows Home + Hyper-V workaround)
# Sizing: 2 startup / 2 min / 4 max dynamic, 2 vCPUs — leaves 4 GB for Windows host

# Tailscale tailnet name
TAILNET_NAME="tailaXXXX.ts.net"

# Hybrid spoke pool name (matches the SpokePool claim name)
HYBRID_SPOKE_NAME="spoke-pool-hybrid-dev-01"

# Hub kubeconfig path (used by join scripts to read spoke kubeconfig)
HUB_KUBECONFIG="${ZERO_OPS_DIR:-$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)}/k8-secrets/kubeconfig/hub-hybrid-dev.kubeconfig"
